<section class="chapter" aria-label="Signals" id="signals">
  <div class="chapter__inner">
    <h2 class="section-title">Signals</h2>
    <p class="muted">Every hiring process generates thousands of data points. Most organizations look at a fraction of them — and mainly after the fact.</p>
    <p>This system tracks three types of signals, including two that traditional people analytics typically ignores.</p>

    <div class="grid3">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Workflow Signals</h3>
        <p class="muted">How <strong>work moves</strong> through the process.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Pass-through rates</li>
          <li>Stage transitions</li>
          <li>Time in stage</li>
          <li>Scheduling delays</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="card">
        <h3>Behavioral Signals</h3>
        <p class="muted">How <strong>candidates perform</strong> inside the process — not how they describe past performance.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Responsiveness to communication</li>
          <li>Attention to detail</li>
          <li>Preparation and follow-through</li>
          <li>Instruction adherence</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="card">
        <h3>Decision Signals</h3>
        <p class="muted">Whether <strong>hiring decisions</strong> themselves are consistent and defensible.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Interviewer scoring variance</li>
          <li>Rubric adherence</li>
          <li>Calibration drift over time</li>
          <li>Override frequency</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout"><b>Signals don't just feed the system. They run the system.</b></div>

    <details class="sys-details">
      <summary>
        <div class="sys-sum">
          <h3>Workflow Signals in depth</h3>
          <p>How work moves through the hiring process and where the system slows down.</p>
          <span class="small">[ Expand ]</span>
        </div>
        <span class="sys-chev">›</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="sys-body">
        <p class="muted">Workflow signals capture how the hiring process operates at a system level. They surface patterns in movement, timing, and coordination that often remain invisible until they become bottlenecks.</p>
        <p>Pass-through rates, cycle times, and scheduling delays reveal how the process operates — not whether candidates are strong or weak.</p>
        <div class="miniGrid miniGrid--4">
          <div class="mini">
            <b>Throughput</b>
            <span>Will pipeline volume get us to our hiring goals on time?</span>
          </div>
          <div class="mini">
            <b>Pass-through rates</b>
            <span>Where in the funnel are candidates being filtered out?</span>
          </div>
          <div class="mini">
            <b>Cycle time</b>
            <span>How quickly does the system move candidates from one decision to the next?</span>
          </div>
          <div class="mini">
            <b>Scheduling delays</b>
            <span>Where is coordination friction slowing the process down?</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="sys-details">
      <summary>
        <div class="sys-sum">
          <h3>Behavioral Signals in depth</h3>
          <p>Observable candidate behavior during the process — not interview impressions after the fact.</p>
          <span class="small">[ Expand ]</span>
        </div>
        <span class="sys-chev">›</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="sys-body">
        <p class="muted">Most hiring systems try to infer how a candidate behaves by asking them to describe past behavior. Behavioral signals work differently — they measure what candidates actually do during the hiring process itself.</p>
        <p>Responsiveness, preparation, attention to detail, and instruction adherence are all observable during the process. When captured consistently, they become structured evidence rather than a feeling someone had in the room.</p>
        <div class="grid2 grid2--equal">
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Traditional approach</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>Candidates describe past behavior</li>
              <li>Interviewers interpret and rate</li>
              <li>Decision relies on a feeling in the room</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Behavioral signal measurement</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>Responsiveness to communication</li>
              <li>Clarity of written communication</li>
              <li>Preparation and follow-through</li>
              <li>Attention to detail and instruction adherence</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout">Hiring doesn't have to be based on a feeling the interviewer had in the moment.</div>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="sys-details">
      <summary>
        <div class="sys-sum">
          <h3>Decision Signals in depth</h3>
          <p>Whether the decisions themselves are consistent, calibrated, and defensible.</p>
          <span class="small">[ Expand ]</span>
        </div>
        <span class="sys-chev">›</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="sys-body">
        <p class="muted">Most recruiting analytics measure throughput — time, volume, conversion. Decision signals measure something different: whether evaluation is consistent, explainable, and stable across reviewers and over time.</p>
        <div class="miniGrid miniGrid--4">
          <div class="mini">
            <b>Decision Consistency</b>
            <span>Do similar candidates get similar outcomes across reviewers?</span>
          </div>
          <div class="mini">
            <b>Rubric Adherence</b>
            <span>Are must-haves actually being applied as must-haves?</span>
          </div>
          <div class="mini">
            <b>Calibration Stability</b>
            <span>Is "what good means" drifting silently over time?</span>
          </div>
          <div class="mini">
            <b>Predictive Strength</b>
            <span>Does the screening signal actually predict downstream outcomes?</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout">Decision signals make evaluation quality measurable — so teams can reduce variance, detect drift, and improve decision hygiene without turning hiring into a black box.</div>
      </div>
    </details>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="chapter" aria-label="Decision Intelligence" id="decision-intelligence">
  <div class="chapter__inner">
    <h2 class="section-title">Decision Intelligence: <span class="accent">The Missing Layer</span></h2>
    <p class="muted">Data and dashboards are widely used. Decisions still rely on gut feel. The missing layer is what sits between them.</p>

    <div class="callout">A <b>decision system</b> connects signals, logic, and action so outcomes become diagnosable and improvable — not just visible.</div>

    <div class="grid5 grid5--persist" aria-label="Architecture blocks">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Data →</h3>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Dashboards →</h3>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3><span class="accent">Decision Intelligence →</span></h3>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Human Decisions →</h3>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Outcomes</h3>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3 style="margin-top:2.5rem;">Metrics</h3>
    <p class="muted">Signals only work if they mean the same thing everywhere. The metrics layer governs definitions, formulas, grain, and ownership so dashboards stay consistent and decisions are auditable.</p>

    <p>Most recruiting analytics measure throughput — time, volume, conversion. The metrics dictionary also tracks decision integrity: whether evaluation is consistent, explainable, and stable over time.</p>

    <div class="miniGrid miniGrid--4">
      <div class="mini">
        <b>Decision Consistency</b>
        <span>Do similar candidates get similar outcomes across reviewers?</span>
      </div>
      <div class="mini">
        <b>Rubric Adherence</b>
        <span>Are must-haves actually being applied as must-haves?</span>
      </div>
      <div class="mini">
        <b>Calibration Stability</b>
        <span>Is "what good means" drifting silently over time?</span>
      </div>
      <div class="mini">
        <b>Predictive Strength</b>
        <span>Does the screening signal actually predict downstream outcomes?</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <details class="sys-details">
      <summary>
        <div class="sys-sum">
          <h3>Metrics in depth</h3>
          <p>Governed definitions, formulas, and decision integrity metrics.</p>
          <span class="small">[ Expand ]</span>
        </div>
        <span class="sys-chev">›</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="sys-body">
        <p class="muted">Most reporting failures are not dashboard problems — they are definition drift. Two teams calculate the same metric differently and the organization stops trusting the numbers.</p>
        <p>One metric name. One definition. One formula. No local interpretations.</p>
        <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Featured metrics">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>ID</th>
                <th>Metric</th>
                <th>Definition</th>
                <th>Formula</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>14.15</td>
                <td>Pass-through %: App → Screen</td>
                <td>Share of applications that progress to recruiter screen.</td>
                <td>moved_to_screen / applications</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>14.16</td>
                <td>Pass-through %: Final → Offer</td>
                <td>Share of final loops that result in an offer.</td>
                <td>offers.sent / final_loops</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>14.18</td>
                <td>Pass-through %: Offer → Hire</td>
                <td>Share of offers that result in a hire.</td>
                <td>hires / offers.sent</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>14.20</td>
                <td>Time-to-Hire (days, median)</td>
                <td>Median days from start of process to hire.</td>
                <td>median(hire_dt − start_dt)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>14.21</td>
                <td>Time-to-Schedule (hours, median)</td>
                <td>Hours from interview request to first scheduled slot.</td>
                <td>median(interview_start − request_dt)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>14.09</td>
                <td>Decision Latency (days, median)</td>
                <td>Days from decision request created to decision recorded.</td>
                <td>median(decision_dt − request_dt)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>14.14</td>
                <td>On-time Scorecards % (≤24h)</td>
                <td>Share of submitted scorecards received within 24 hours.</td>
                <td>scorecards_≤24h / scorecards</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>14.08</td>
                <td>Candidate NPS</td>
                <td>Net Promoter Score from post-process candidate survey.</td>
                <td>(promoters − detractors) / total * 100</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <div class="ctaRow ctaRow--left">
          <a class="btn primary" href="/system/metrics/dictionary/">Full Metrics Dictionary →</a>
          <a class="btn" href="/assets/documents/metricsv2.csv">Download CSV →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </details>

    <h3 style="margin-top:2.5rem;">Decision Logic</h3>
    <p class="muted">Signals need to mean something operationally. Decision logic defines what a pattern implies, when action is triggered, and where automation stops and human judgment takes over.</p>

    <p>Not every signal warrants the same response. The system uses four autonomy tiers to route action based on risk level — so low-stakes work moves fast and high-stakes decisions always reach a human.</p>

    <div class="miniGrid miniGrid--4">
      <div class="mini">
        <b>Tier 0 — Monitor</b>
        <span>Surface the signal. Measure, detect drift, take no action.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="mini">
        <b>Tier 1 — Auto-execute</b>
        <span>Low-risk reminders, routing, and hygiene. Logged and visible.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="mini">
        <b>Tier 2 — Recommend</b>
        <span>AI proposes an action. A human approves it before anything happens.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="mini">
        <b>Tier 3 — Escalate</b>
        <span>High-stakes outcomes always route to humans. No exceptions.</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <details class="sys-details">
      <summary>
        <div class="sys-sum">
          <h3>Decision logic in depth</h3>
          <p>Thresholds, routing rules, decision rights, and receipts.</p>
          <span class="small">[ Expand ]</span>
        </div>
        <span class="sys-chev">›</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="sys-body">
        <p class="muted">Without decision logic, two people looking at the same signal invent two different next steps. With explicit logic, the system interprets comparable inputs in comparable ways.</p>
        <div class="grid2 grid2--equal">
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Thresholds</h3>
            <p class="muted">Define when a signal should trigger attention, intervention, or escalation.</p>
            <ul>
              <li>Expected ranges</li>
              <li>Alert triggers</li>
              <li>Escalation boundaries</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Routing Rules</h3>
            <p class="muted">Define where work goes once a condition is met.</p>
            <ul>
              <li>Who owns the next step</li>
              <li>Whether action is automated or approved</li>
              <li>What gets logged for auditability</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="grid2 grid2--equal">
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Decision Rights</h3>
            <p class="muted">Not every action belongs to the same actor. Authority is explicit, not assumed.</p>
            <ul>
              <li>AI may observe, recommend, or act</li>
              <li>Humans retain authority where stakes are material</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Receipts</h3>
            <p class="muted">Every action is traceable to the signal and logic that produced it.</p>
            <ul>
              <li>Why this surfaced</li>
              <li>What threshold was crossed</li>
              <li>What action was taken</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </details>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="chapter" aria-label="Actions" id="actions">
  <div class="chapter__inner">
    <h2 class="section-title">Actions</h2>
    <p class="muted">The system only works if insight turns into action and outcomes feed the model back.</p>

    <div class="grid3" aria-label="Operating loop">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Interpret</h3>
        <p class="muted">Signals are normalized and evaluated against shared definitions and expected ranges.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Metrics stay comparable</li>
          <li>Drift becomes visible</li>
          <li>Signal quality improves over time</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Route</h3>
        <p class="muted">The system decides whether to monitor, recommend, auto-execute, or escalate.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Low-risk work moves fast</li>
          <li>Higher-stakes decisions route to humans</li>
          <li>Escalation is rule-based, not improvised</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Learn</h3>
        <p class="muted">Outcomes feed back into the operating model so tools do not drift apart over time.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Closed-loop learning</li>
          <li>Threshold refinement</li>
          <li>Change control over shared logic</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

